# [Find Subtree Sizes After Changes](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-subtree-sizes-after-changes/)

## Approach: DFS + Tree + Ancestor Tracking

1. Construct an adjacency list using the parent array to represent the original tree.
2. Maintain a `prev` array of size 26 to track the nearest ancestor having the same character.
3. Perform DFS from the root while updating the nearest ancestor for each character.
4. For every node, restore the previous character occurrence after processing its children. This ensures sibling subtrees are handled independently.
5. If an ancestor with the same character exists, update the node's parent to that ancestor.
6. Initially, every node has a subtree size of 1. After processing its children, add its subtree size to its updated parent.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>>node;
    vector<int>prev;
    vector<int>subtrees;

    void dfs(string&s,int curr_index,vector<int>&parent){
        char ch = s[curr_index];
        int prev_index = prev[ch - 'a'];

        prev[ch - 'a'] = curr_index;

        for(const int&child : node[curr_index])
            dfs(s,child,parent);

        prev[ch - 'a'] = prev_index;

        if(curr_index != 0){
            int newParent = parent[curr_index];

            if(prev_index != -1)
                newParent = prev_index;

            parent[curr_index] = newParent;
            subtrees[newParent] += subtrees[curr_index];
        }
    }

    vector<int> findSubtreeSizes(vector<int>& parent, string s) {
        int n = parent.size();

        node.assign(n,{});
        prev.assign(26,-1);
        subtrees.assign(n,1);

        for(int i=1;i<n;i++)
            node[parent[i]].push_back(i);

        dfs(s,0,parent);

        return subtrees;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(N)\) — Each node is visited exactly once.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(N)\) — Adjacency list, subtree sizes, and recursion stack.
